h one was deployed this morning.\n\n**What I checked:**\n- There's no service called just `payments`. Two payments services exist, and both have the same error rate in all three regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west):\n\n| Service | Current version | Health | Error rate | Last healthy release in production |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `payments-api` | v5.1.0 | degraded | 18% | v5.0.0 |\n| `payments-worker` | v3.4.0 | unhealthy | 18% | v3.3.0 |\n\n- The release history has no timestamps, so I can't tell which release was this morning's.\n- There are no open incidents right now.\n\n**Why I stopped:** You asked me to roll back one service. Rolling back the wrong one would change production without fixing the problem, so I'd rather check with you first. Both rollbacks would go to versions that were healthy in production, so neither needs change-manager approval.\n\n**Which should I do?**\n1. Roll back `payments-api` to v5.0.0\n2. Roll back `payments-worker` to v3.3.0\n3. Roll back both, since both are failing\n\nOnce you choose, I'll do the rollback and open the SEV2 incident for that service straight away. If you want the incident opened now, I can do that first and note that the root cause isn't confirmed yet.", "type": "text"}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01PYLtH3BYocEbZ9NhiyLcsx", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "version": "v3.3.0", "confidence": 0.93}, "name": "rollback", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01MHkeVtnAft1tXioyA33nC4", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"title": "payments-worker errors after v3.4.0 deploy; rolled back to v3.3.0", "severity": "SEV2", "service": "payments-worker", "summary": "payments-worker v3.4.0 (this morning's deploy) went unhealthy with an 18% error rate in us-east, us-west and eu-west. payments-api (v5.1.0, degraded, 18% errors) is failing downstream of the worker. We are rolling payments-worker back to v3.3.0, its last release that was healthy in production. Next: confirm that the worker and API error rates recover after the rollback.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-rollback-sev2", "confidence": 0.92}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_012feAZMTraSEqaW2d8ddfAY", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_018NfsRBssTnJqJsGPpRUv7w", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled `payments-worker` back to v3.3.0 and opened the SEV2. The worker has recovered, but `payments-api` is still showing errors.\n\n- **Rollback:** `payments-worker` is on v3.3.0 in us-east, us-west and eu-west. It's healthy again and its error rate dropped from 18% to 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** I opened **INC-0001** (SEV2, `payments-worker`), which paged on-call. It records the bad v3.4.0 deploy, the rollback and the downstream impact on the API.\n- **Still a problem:** `payments-api` is still on v5.1.0, marked degraded, with an 18% error rate when I checked right after the rollback.\n\nIf the API errors were only coming from the worker, they should start falling soon. It may just need a moment to catch up, or v5.1.0 itself may have a separate problem. I'd check `payments-api` again in a few minutes. If its error rate hasn't moved, the next step would be rolling it back to v5.0.0, its last release that was healthy in production. I haven't touched it since you said the worker was the cause. Let me know if you want me to keep watching it or go ahead with that rollback.", "type": "text"}]}]}